//! [`HipForward`] — the forward pass on a card, behind a channel.
//!
//! # Why a thread
//!
//! Every HIP handle in `saber-hip` is `!Send` by construction and
//! [`StepExecutor`](saber_sched::StepExecutor) is `Send`, because the scheduler's step loop owns it
//! on a plain `std::thread`. Both cannot hold for one struct with a `Stream` in it. So the device
//! side — plans, arenas, weights, session state, the stream, the captured graphs — lives on **one
//! thread of its own**, which is also `ARCHITECTURE.md`'s rule ("confine every HIP call to one OS
//! thread, structurally"), and [`HipForward`] is a channel handle to it.
//!
//! # What this engine owns, and what it does not
//!
//! Owns: one body plan per **planned shape** (the prefill row counts the scheduler's chunking rule
//! emits, plus one decode shape per batch), one head plan per shape, the arenas behind them, every
//! weight bound by name out of the TP=1 device-final pack, the KV block pool, the GDN state slots,
//! one index blob rewritten each step, and one **captured graph per decode batch**.
//!
//! Does not own: which sequence runs when, how many blocks it gets, what the verify width is. That
//! is `saber-sched`, and the point of the seam is that this file cannot second-guess it.
//!
//! # Batch > 1, and what makes the padding harmless
//!
//! A decode step at the `b8s1` bucket is eight rows whether or not eight sequences are running,
//! because a captured graph has a fixed `M`. Every index table is eight wide and each entry is that
//! *sequence's* own fact; the rows past the live slots are padding, and what makes them safe is
//! structural rather than a promise that nobody reads them: the null GDN state slot (both decode
//! kernels bail on `slot <= 0`), a zero `cu` span (so `slen == 0`), and `seqused_k == 0` (both
//! attention kernels open with `if (ctx <= 0) return;`). See [`crate::index`].
//!
//! # Why a decode step is one graph, and why that is safe here
//!
//! A replay re-runs the same kernels over the same addresses. Everything that varies between two
//! decode steps of the same bucket is *contents*, not addresses: the index blob is one allocation
//! rewritten in place, the arenas and the state buffers are fixed, and the token buffer is fixed.
//! The GDN and KV caches are rewritten in place, which is exactly what the next step of the same
//! sequence wants — `block_capture`'s warning about restoring state between replays is about
//! replaying a step *for the same token twice*, which a serving engine never does. So the graph is
//! captured once per batch at startup and replayed per step, with the H2D of the index blob and the
//! D2H of the tokens outside it. The eager path stays, behind a counted which-path flag, and the gate
//! runs the same prompt down both.
//!
//! # TP = 2: the same engine, twice, one card each
//!
//! At `world = 2` this file runs on **both** cards, in two processes, from the two halves of a TP=2
//! device-final pack. Rank 0 is the [`Leader`](crate::tp2::PeerRole::Leader) — it holds the
//! scheduler and the serve plane and is the only rank anything above the seam talks to — and rank 1
//! is the [`Follower`](crate::tp2::PeerRole::Follower): the same code, the same plans, the same
//! captured graphs, driven one [`Command`](crate::tp2::Command) per step over the pair's socket.
//!
//! Three things change, and they are all consequences of the planner refusing to guess a rank:
//!
//! 1. **The embedding and the head are hand-resolved.** `BlockPlan` refuses `Site::Embedding` above
//!    TP=1 (the vocab-parallel slice needs a rank the plan does not carry) and refuses `Site::Head`
//!    because the graph's `logits.gather` has no code object. So the body plan is layers only and
//!    this file resolves the embedding, the final norm and `lm_head` itself, exactly as
//!    `saber step-tp2` does.
//! 2. **The 129 all-reduces are nodes.** Every `layer.*.all_reduce` the planner routed to
//!    `Route::Identity` is replaced by the real descriptor over the same address — the identity's
//!    alias *is* the buffer an in-place reduce wants — and the embedding's reduce is added in front.
//!    They go INSIDE the captured graph, which is legal only because both ranks replay the same
//!    graph the same number of times in the same order: `saber_exec::PeerCapture::Lockstep` is the
//!    name of that assertion and [`capture_step`] is where it is passed. What it costs is paid
//!    here: every peer-visible region is prepared and zeroed before either capture opens, every
//!    collective's launch digest is agreed on the HOST before it (a graph cannot rendezvous), and
//!    every wait is deadline-bounded.
//! 3. **The argmax is local and the gather is on the host.** `lm_head` is column-parallel, so each
//!    rank's argmax wins only its own half of the vocabulary. The two ranks swap
//!    `(column, logit)` per row and [`crate::tp2::combine`] picks — 16 bytes per row per step
//!    instead of a 300 KiB shard, and counted.
//!
//! # SPEC = 0
//!
//! The drafter is absent: one row per sequence. That is the P3 comparator's shape and the only one
//! whose GDN state geometry the graph's `bytes_per_slot` covers — the conv cache is
//! `[slots][qkv_n][conv_width - 1 + q_len - 1]` and the declared slot size assumes `q_len = 1`. A
//! wider step is refused rather than overrunning the slot.
//!
//! # A prefill chunk runs at its bucket's rows, and its ATTENTION runs at its own
//!
//! A 234-token prompt is one `p2048` chunk, so the pass pads the ids up to 2048 and runs the planned
//! shape. `cu` carries the **live** count, so the GDN scan stops at the real tokens, and the padding
//! rows cost GEMM time and are never read. The alternative — a plan per prompt length — is an arena
//! per prompt length, and `BlockExecutor` cannot be handed an existing allocation (listed in the
//! branch report as an API this crate wants).
//!
//! The attention is the one op that cannot take the bucket's row count: `saber_attn_prefill` reads
//! `q_len` as the live predicate and as the right-alignment of its mask, so at the bucket's count
//! every padded row is a live query row computed out of the previous request's arena, and
//! `wave_any` lets it steer the last live row's softmax rescale (ws-l16). Every prefill step
//! therefore points its plan at the chunk (`BlockPlan::retarget_prefill_rows`) and tells the index
//! law which number that is (`AttnQLen::Exact`), so `seqused_k` and the kernarg cannot drift apart
//! — ws-h27, and `--no-live-rows` is the control that puts the old pair back.

